# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Board data and secrets
# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# ONE DATASET IS COMMITTED ON PURPOSE, and it is the only one.
#
# `/dataset/` used to be ignored outright. The instruction that changed it:
# "commit one dataset to the repo as the default so the deployed app works for
# someone opening it cold, then upload becomes the way to swap rather than the
# only way in." Without it a cold visitor meets the 409/needsDataset path and
# has to be handed a .zip before the app does anything at all.
#
# It is safe to commit because BOTH ORGANISATIONS IN THIS EXERCISE ARE
# FICTIONAL — the dataset's own README says so in as many words. Nothing here
# is a real director. That is the whole reason this line could be relaxed, and
# it is the first thing to re-check if a real dataset ever arrives: real board
# attendance is personal data and would go straight back behind the ignore.
#
# `/dataset-*/` STAYS IGNORED, so dataset-b cannot be committed by accident.
/dataset-*/
# The dataset's own README describes BOTH organisations, including the second
# one by name and sector. Committing it would put dataset-b's identity in the
# repo and spoil a test whose entire value is being run blind.
/dataset/README.md
/data/private/
*.dataset.json

# A zipped dataset is still the dataset. Uploading one leaves an archive lying
# in the working tree, and it is only ever one `git add -A` away from being
# committed. Matching the file suffix cannot catch src/lib/dataset/, so this one
# does not need anchoring.
dataset*.zip

